  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nLow-level stratus is present over most of the state, except the\nfar southwest. So far, dense fog generally hasn't materialized \nin the west with most visibility remaining in excess of a mile, \nthough occasional dips below have been observed. Winds over \nwestern North Dakota do maintain a southeasterly component, \nwhich is often favorable for dense fog development. However, \ncombined with near-surface winds that are breezy more often than\nnot, they may just be a tad too strong for more widespread \ndense fog development. Instead favoring limited visibility \nreductions and low-level stratus. Nevertheless, cannot rule out \ndense fog development over parts of the west and especially \nsouthwest as sunrise approaches. The current NBM forecast has \nlow-level stratus eroding through the day today as surface high \npressure begins to filter into the eastern Dakotas and \nMinnesota. However, considering the pervasiveness of current \ncloud cover and the time of year, that may be a tall order, \nespecially for the moister western half of the state.\n\nAforementioned surface high will continue filtering into\nMinnesota with its influence extending over the eastern half of\nthe Dakotas through Saturday. Simultaneously, a well amplified \nthermal ridge over the Eastern Rockies/High Plains will \nmaintain northerly flow aloft over the region. Thus, a moderate\ntemperature gradient will continue today through Saturday with \nhighs ranging from the mid 30s east to low 50s west. As the \nthermal ridge continues sliding eastward Sunday into early next \nweek, a statewide warming trend (moderately east, only \nmarginally west) is favored to reach a crescendo Monday. After \nwhich, an incoming west CONUS trough is favored to flatten the \nridge and bring a statewide cooling trend. Depending on how this\nplays out, could see an extended pattern shift towards the end \nof next week that brings a continued cooling trend and the \npotential return of more organized precipitation. \n\nThe one last thing to note is that as previously mentioned\nsurface high pressure filters into Minnesota; low pressure will\nalso sit along the lee of the Rockies. This tightening pressure\ngradient will result in breezy southeasterly winds Friday mainly\nalong and west of Highway 83, and then across most of the state\nSaturday, with a few stronger gusts possible in western North\nDakota. As the surface high moves off to the southeast, lighter\nwinds are then expected to start next week.",
